<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/18th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Treaty · Peace</p><h1 class="enc-title">Treaty of Belgrade</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1739">1739</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austro-russian-turkish-war-1735-1739">Austro-Russian–Turkish War (1735–1739)</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Treaty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgrade">Belgrade</a>, signed in September 1739, ended the war of 1735–1739 in which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> fought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a>. After a badly managed campaign, Austria returned Belgrade, northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oltenia">Oltenia</a>, which it had gained at Passarowitz in 1718. In a parallel settlement Russia kept <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/azov">Azov</a> but had to demolish its fortifications and keep no fleet on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a>, and Kabarda was declared a neutral barrier.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/treaty-of-belgrade.jpg" alt="Treaty of Belgrade" width="320" height="236" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Belgrade_Treaty_1739.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>In the last stage of the war between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg Monarchy</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a>, the main Habsburg army under Field Marshal Wallis suffered a strategic defeat at the Battle of Grocka on 21 and 22 July 1739. It retreated into the Belgrade Fortress, which Ottoman forces soon besieged. On 31 July the government in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a> took Wallis's powers to negotiate from him and gave them to Count Neipperg, who reached the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grand-vizier">Grand Vizier</a>'s camp on 18 August with instructions approved by Emperor Charles VI. There he found himself increasingly isolated and dependent on the French mediator, Marquis Louis Sauveur de Villeneuve.</p><p>On 1 September Neipperg agreed to a preliminary peace with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a>. His report reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a> on 7 September and provoked outrage, because he had signed without knowing that the Emperor had sent him new instructions on 31 August and that his powers had been revoked on 3 September. Only on returning to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgrade">Belgrade</a> did he learn this, and it was too late to reverse. Further negotiations led to the final treaty, concluded on 18 September in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grand-vizier">Grand Vizier</a>'s camp near Belgrade.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Terms</h2><p>By the treaty the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburgs</a> ceded three regions to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a>: the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banat">Banat</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/craiova">Craiova</a>, in modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oltenia">Oltenia</a>, the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a> with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgrade">Belgrade</a>, and the Bosnian part of Posavina. All of them had come to the Habsburg Monarchy by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-passarowitz">Treaty of Passarowitz</a> in 1718 and had remained under its rule until 1739. The new border was set along the rivers <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sava">Sava</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a>. The territorial changes were substantial and favoured the Ottoman side. The treaty is also noted as one of the last international treaties written in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The treaty ended the existence of the Habsburg-held Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a>, which had lasted since 1718. The withdrawal of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> forces was followed by a large Serbian migration out of the ceded lands. They stayed under Ottoman rule until the next Habsburg-Ottoman war of 1788 to 1791, when Habsburg forces, supported by Serbian rebels, occupied them temporarily in 1788 and 1789.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> decision to negotiate in August 1739 forced its ally <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> to accept talks as well, through French mediation. A separate preliminary treaty was signed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgrade">Belgrade</a> on 18 September and finalised by the Treaty of Niš on 3 October, ending the Russo-Turkish war. After ratification, the instruments were exchanged and a final convention was signed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> on 28 December 1739, again through French mediation. Meanwhile Neipperg and Wallis were arrested and accused over the unfavourable outcome; both were pardoned only after the Emperor's death in 1740.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Treaty of Belgrade” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>Treaty of Belgrade</b> of 1739 declared Kabarda a neutral barrier between Russia and the Ottoman Empire, but by the Treaty of Küçük Kaynarca of 1774 the Ottomans recognised Kabarda as Russian, and in the early nineteenth century Russian forts and Cossack settlements cut deep into its lands.</blockquote><a href="/empire/circassia">Circassia · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Habsburg defeat in the war of 1737 to 1739 led to the <b>Treaty of Belgrade</b> in 1739, which returned Oltenia to Wallachia under Ottoman suzerainty.</blockquote><a href="/state/banat-of-craiova">Banat of Craiova</a></li></ul></section>
